+\begin{abstract}
+Function vectors \citep{todd2024function} and task vectors \citep{hendel2023incontext} make a similar claim from different directions: in-context learning compresses a task into a single transportable activation vector. Both claims rest on 2023-era models, mostly GPT-J-6B and Llama-2, and the two methods have never been compared on the same tasks, models, and controls. We reproduce both in one harness on word-pair tasks drawn from a 16-task suite, across GPT-J-6B (the original model for both papers), Llama-3.1-8B, Gemma-2-9b-it, and Llama-3.1-70B, using NNsight with NDIF remote execution for all model-internal access; per-model task coverage varied with deployment availability and is reported in full. Task vectors replicate on all four models, recovering 0.80 of the in-context accuracy gap at 70B (task-clustered 95\% CI $[0.68, 0.90]$; 0.68 under cross-validated layer selection). Our port of the function-vector recipe replicates on GPT-J ($d=2.4$ against matched controls) but produces nothing on Gemma-2-9b-it: recovery 0.007 across 12 (task, seed) pairs at every injection layer, with probe-task diagnostics showing that injection strengths up to $8\times$ and a basis correction for Gemma's post-attention normalization change nothing, while a positive control on the same write path perturbs the model. A control neither paper ran reframes the task-vector story: a vector extracted from label-shuffled demonstrations recovers a third to half of the gap on average, and per-task the deflation ranges from total (the vector carries the input-output mapping) to none (it carries only task identity), with the same task landing differently on different models. Code, data, and a full deviation log are public.\footnote{\url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/fv-tv-repro-7D8E}}
+\end{abstract}
