+\section{Introduction}
+\label{sec:intro}
+
+A model given ten input-output demonstrations somehow becomes a different function for the rest of the prompt. Two well-cited papers argue this happens through a compact intermediate object. \citet{todd2024function} find a small set of attention heads whose summed outputs form a \emph{function vector} (FV): add it to the residual stream of a zero-shot prompt and the model performs the task. \citet{hendel2023incontext} patch a single hidden state, the \emph{task vector} (TV), from a demonstration prompt into a zero-shot forward pass with much the same effect. The two mechanisms differ in where they read, what they write, and how they intervene. The claims are nonetheless close cousins, published a week apart, and never tested against each other.
+
+Both evidence bases are also narrow by 2026 standards. Todd et al.\ report GPT-J-6B, GPT-NeoX, and Llama-2; Hendel et al.\ report GPT-J, Pythia, and LLaMA-1-era models. Interpretability findings have a documented habit of not surviving contact with new models and datasets \citep{zhang2024towards,yin2025which}, which is the concern this workshop track exists to address.
+
+We ask a plain question: do these two claims hold on the same tasks, under the same controls, on models neither paper touched? (Todd et al.\ did reach 70B with Llama-2; Hendel et al.\ stopped at 30B, so the 70B task-vector arm is new territory for that claim only.) Concretely:
+
+\begin{itemize}
+    \item We re-implement both methods in a single NNsight \citep{fiottokaufman2025nnsight} harness and rerun them on GPT-J-6B, reproducing the original setting for both papers before changing anything.
+    \item We extend both methods to Llama-3.1-8B and Gemma-2-9b-it, and task vectors to Llama-3.1-70B via NDIF remote execution, with matched random-vector, random-head, and shuffled-label controls, bootstrap confidence intervals, and effect sizes.
+    \item We report one clean failure: our function-vector port does nothing on Gemma-2-9b-it while task vectors work on it, and we instrument the null with a positive control, norm diagnostics, an injection-strength sweep, and a basis correction.
+    \item We quantify something neither paper measured: a task vector built from label-shuffled demonstrations recovers a third to half of the ICL gap on average, with per-task deflation ranging from total to none depending on how much of the task the model already knows.
+\end{itemize}
+
+Negative and qualified results are results. Our framing follows the reproducibility-report conventions of the ML Reproducibility Challenge \citep{pineau2021improving}: claims are enumerated, each gets its own verdict, and every deviation from the original protocols is logged (Appendix~\ref{sec:deviations}).
+
+\section{Background and Claims Under Test}
+\label{sec:claims}
+
+\paragraph{Function vectors.} For a task presented as 10-shot ICL, \citet{todd2024function} compute each attention head's mean output over many demonstration prompts, then measure each head's causal contribution by patching that mean into label-shuffled prompts and reading the recovery of the correct answer (average indirect effect, AIE). The FV is the sum of the top-$k$ heads' mean outputs ($k=10$), projected into the residual stream. Added to the residual stream at an early-middle layer of a zero-shot prompt, it restores much of the model's ICL accuracy.
+
+\paragraph{Task vectors.} \citet{hendel2023incontext} take the residual-stream hidden state $\theta$ at the last token of a demonstration prompt (with a dummy query), at an intermediate layer $L$. Patching $\theta$ into position at layer $L$ of a zero-shot forward pass recovers most ICL accuracy, peaking at intermediate layers and collapsing late.
+
+FVs are additive and head-derived; TVs replace a state wholesale. FV construction needs a causal search over heads; TV construction needs one forward pass. Related comparisons exist from other angles: \citet{brumley2024comparing} compare FV-style steering against alternatives behaviorally, \citet{yin2025which} ask which heads drive ICL across twelve models (none of them Gemma), \citet{liu2024incontext} extract in-context vectors by a different construction, and \citet{bakalova2025contextualize} show ICL circuitry in the Gemma-2 family is findable, which makes an FV-shaped mechanism's absence there (\S\ref{sec:gemma}) a sharper question. Table~\ref{tab:claims} lists the specific claims we test, using identifiers that recur in Section~\ref{sec:results} and in the released code.
