+\subsection{Interventions}
+
+Prompts follow the shared format \texttt{Q: \{x\}\textbackslash nA: \{y\}} with a trailing unterminated \texttt{Q: \{query\}\textbackslash nA:}. Per (task, seed): the ICL ceiling is 10-shot accuracy and the zero-shot floor is 0-shot accuracy on the same evaluation split. For FVs, we compute mean head activations over 32 fresh 10-shot prompts, run the AIE search (10 label-shuffled trials; 5 on Gemma-2, see Appendix~\ref{sec:deviations}), build the FV from the top 10 heads, and sweep additive injection over every second layer. For TVs, we extract $\theta$ at all layers from one demonstration prompt with a held-out dummy query and sweep patching over every second layer (every third at 70B). The headline statistic is the \emph{recovery ratio}: $(\text{acc}_{\text{method}} - \text{acc}_{\text{zero}}) / (\text{acc}_{\text{ICL}} - \text{acc}_{\text{zero}})$ at the best swept layer.
+
+\subsection{Controls and Statistics}
+
+Each method gets matched controls evaluated at its best layer: a norm-matched random Gaussian vector and a random-$k$-heads FV for the FV arm, and a $\theta$ extracted from label-shuffled demonstrations for the TV arm. Seeds use fresh demonstration and evaluation splits. Because (task, seed) pairs cluster within task, all confidence intervals are task-clustered bootstraps (10{,}000 resamples of tasks, seeds pooled within); Cohen's $d$ is reported against the pooled matched controls. A claim is graded \emph{pass} when the method's clustered CI excludes its matched control's mean and $d \geq 0.8$; \emph{weak pass} when the CI excludes the control but $0.5 \leq d < 0.8$; \emph{insufficient data} when coverage is below 5 tasks; \emph{fail} when the method is indistinguishable from its control.
+
+Max-over-layers selection on the evaluation split inflates the method estimate relative to a control evaluated at one layer, so we co-report a cross-validated estimate: the best layer is chosen on seed 0 and evaluated on the remaining seeds. Inflation varies by arm (0.00 on GPT-J TV to 0.14 on Gemma-2 TV) and changes no verdict. Todd et al.\ select layers by sweep; Hendel et al.\ select on a development set, which corresponds to our cross-validated variant, so their headline numbers are not subject to this inflation.
+
+\subsection{Compute}
+\label{sec:compute}
+
+Everything ran through NDIF remote execution from a laptop with no usable GPU; models stay resident on NDIF's shared deployments and each traced forward pass is one remote job. Measured throughput was roughly 18 seconds per job including queueing and result download, which made job count, not FLOPs, the binding constraint. The AIE head search dominates: on Gemma-2 (42 layers, 256k vocabulary, and a tight per-process memory allowance on its deployment) a single full-specification (task, seed) pair costs about 1{,}400 jobs. That is what motivated 25-item evaluation splits, 10 AIE trials, strided injection sweeps, and the 70B TV-only scope. The reported experiments consumed roughly 21{,}000 remote jobs over three days, all on shared no-cost infrastructure.
